---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro'
import KaTeX from '../components/KaTeX.astro'
---
<SiteLayout
  title="Marici · Physics from one object"
  description="A single 4-point carrier with automorphism group S4 and overlap matrix G_ij gives quantum mechanics, general relativity, the Standard Model, and connections across mathematics."
>
  <section class="frontier-section" id="carrier-results" aria-labelledby="carrier-title" style="margin-top:2rem;">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Marici relational carrier</p><h2 id="carrier-title">Relational carrier: physics from one object</h2></div>
                <p>A single 4-point carrier <KaTeX tex="\\mathrm{Bool} \\times \\mathrm{Bool}" /> with automorphism group <KaTeX tex="S_4" /> and overlap matrix <KaTeX tex="G_{ij} = \\langle f_j \\mid f_i \\rangle" /> gives quantum mechanics, general relativity, the Standard Model, and connections across mathematics — all from the same overlap structure.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:1.5rem; max-width:620px; margin:0 auto;">
        <table class="dimension-table"><tr><td class="dt-role">Physics</td><td class="dt-name">Connected</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" class="dt-body">QM, GR, SM (8 sectors), cosmology (5 epochs), Larmor, Spin, Beta decay, EPR, Mach, <KaTeX tex="\\Omega_b, \\Omega_{\\mathrm{DM}}, \\Omega_{\\mathrm{de}}" /></td></tr></table>
        <table class="dimension-table"><tr><td class="dt-role">Mathematics</td><td class="dt-name">Connected</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" class="dt-body">Boolean algebra, CFT, category theory, algebraic geometry, modular forms, homotopy theory, knot theory, TQFT, division algebras, ML, and more</td></tr></table>
        <table class="dimension-table"><tr><td class="dt-role">Constants</td><td class="dt-name">Derived</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" class="dt-body"><KaTeX tex="137, \\quad \\sin^2\\theta = \\frac{3}{13}, \\quad \\text{Yukawa} \\times 6, \\quad m_H = 125\\,\\mathrm{GeV}, \\quad \\Lambda_{\\mathrm{QCD}}, \\quad m_p = 938\\,\\mathrm{MeV}, \\quad \\frac{m_p}{m_e} = 1836" /><br /><KaTeX tex="\\delta_{\\mathrm{CKM}} \\ (\\sim 1.3\\%), \\quad \\delta_{\\mathrm{PMNS}} \\ (\\text{exact}), \\quad y_t = 1, \\quad \\Lambda, \\quad \\frac{M_{\\mathrm{Pl}}}{v} = 11^{15} \\times 12 \\times Z" /><br /><KaTeX tex="\\Omega_b = \\frac{6}{121}, \\quad \\Omega_{\\mathrm{DM}} = \\frac{6}{23}, \\quad \\Omega_{\\mathrm{de}} = \\frac{11}{16}" /></td></tr></table>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex; gap:1rem; margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <a class="primary-action" href="/results/">View the complete results</a>
        <a class="text-action" href="/results/">View the full results &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="snapshot-callout" id="bridges" aria-labelledby="bridges-title" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
    <p class="eyebrow">Cross-sector bridges</p>
    <h2 id="bridges-title">Postnikov descent</h2>
    <p>QM ↔ GR, couplings ↔ masses, Planck ↔ weak, DM ↔ DE ↔ baryons, Mach ↔ GR, baryogenesis ↔ PMNS, Larmor ↔ Newton ↔ Einstein, flavor ↔ gauge, CC ↔ holography, control theory ↔ Machian bootstrap. The same carrier descent, the same four Gram numbers, the same Postnikov tower at every bridge.</p>
    <a class="text-action" href="/results/#bridges">Read the full Postnikov descent &rarr;</a>
  </section>

  <section class="snapshot-callout" id="control" aria-labelledby="control-title" style="margin-top:1rem;">
    <p class="eyebrow">Control theory reframing</p>
    <h2 id="control-title">The universe as a feedback system</h2>
    <p>The four Gram numbers are pole locations; <KaTeX tex="\\varepsilon = \\frac{1}{90} - \\frac{1}{5280}" /> is the feedback gain; <KaTeX tex="Z = \\frac{1}{1+\\varepsilon}" /> is the closed-loop transfer function. The integral term drives <KaTeX tex="\\Omega_k \\to 0" />, the fibration phases are Nyquist margins, and the flavor puzzle is an LQR Riccati equation. The Gram-based constants and dynamics expressed in the language of classical control.</p>
    <a class="text-action" href="/results/#control">Read the full control theory reframing &rarr;</a>
  </section>

    <section class="section-shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Falsifiable predictions</p><h2 id="predictions-title">Predictions</h2></div>
        <p>Each prediction is a rational expression in <KaTeX tex="11, 12, 4, 10" />. Most predictions have no free parameters; <KaTeX tex="\\delta_{\\mathrm{CKM}}" /> has a <KaTeX tex="\\pi/3" /> base angle that is numerically close but not Gram-derived. The experiments that can confirm or falsify them exist or are under construction.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="dimension-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr; max-width:620px; margin:0 auto;">
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">CMB-S4 / Simons Obs.</p><h3><KaTeX tex="\\Omega_k = \\frac{91}{44528} = 0.002" /></h3></div></header><p>The cosmic budget from Gram ratios leaves a deficit of <KaTeX tex="\\frac{91}{44528}" /> for spatial curvature. Planck 2018 gives <KaTeX tex="\\Omega_k = -0.001 \\pm 0.002" /> — <KaTeX tex="\\Omega_k = +0.002" /> is within 1.5σ. Next-generation CMB experiments at 0.1% precision will resolve this.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">DUNE / Hyper-K</p><h3><KaTeX tex="\\delta_{\\mathrm{PMNS}} = \\frac{12}{10}\\pi = 216^\\circ" /></h3></div></header><p>Exact Gram expression. Current global fits place <KaTeX tex="\\delta_{\\mathrm{PMNS}}" /> near <KaTeX tex="220^\\circ" />; DUNE and Hyper-Kamiokande will measure it to <KaTeX tex="\\pm 5^\\circ" /> within a decade.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">LHCb / Belle II</p><h3><KaTeX tex="\\delta_{\\mathrm{CKM}} \\approx \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\left(\\frac{4}{12}\\right)^2 = 66.4^\\circ" /></h3></div></header><p>Within 1.3% of observed <KaTeX tex="65.5^\\circ" />. The <KaTeX tex="(4/12)^2" /> term is Gram-derived; the base <KaTeX tex="\\pi/3" /> is numerically close but not Gram-derived. LHCb and Belle II will refine to sub-degree precision.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Penning traps</p><h3><KaTeX tex="\\frac{m_p}{m_e} = 12 \\times (12^2 + 3^2) = 1836" /></h3></div></header><p>Already within 0.01% of observed <KaTeX tex="1836.15" />. Deviations would reveal new physics in the carrier overlap structure.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">HL-LHC / FCC</p><h3><KaTeX tex="m_H = 11^2 + 4 = 125\\,\\mathrm{GeV}" /></h3></div></header><p>Current measurement <KaTeX tex="125.1\\,\\mathrm{GeV}" /> is within 0.08%. Future colliders measure to <KaTeX tex="10\\,\\mathrm{MeV}" /> precision, testing at 0.01%.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Space-based tests</p><h3>Machian <KaTeX tex="G" /> varies at <KaTeX tex="10^{-4}" /> level</h3></div></header><p>The bootstrap <KaTeX tex="Z = \\frac{1}{1 + 1/90 - 1/5280}" /> predicts <KaTeX tex="G" /> is shielded by all matter. MICROSCOPE follow-on and atom interferometry can detect this.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Dark matter mechanism</p><h3>Particle DM <i>vs</i> Machian inertia</h3></div></header><p><KaTeX tex="\\Omega_{\\mathrm{DM}} = \\frac{6}{23} = 0.2609" /> (observed <KaTeX tex="0.264" />, 1.2% error) fits both sterile neutrinos (X-ray lines) and modified inertia (scale-dependent <KaTeX tex="G" />). X-ray lines confirm particles; scale-dependent <KaTeX tex="G" /> with no X-ray lines confirms Machian inertia. Both cannot be true.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex; gap:1rem; margin-top:1.5rem; justify-content:center;">
        <a class="primary-action" href="/results/">View the complete results & predictions</a>
      </div>
    </section>

  <section class="section-shell" aria-labelledby="theory-link-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Full derivation</p>
    <h2 id="theory-link-title">From <KaTeX tex="\\mathrm{Bool} \\times \\mathrm{Bool}" /> to physics: the relational carrier: read the derivation.</h2>
    <p>Derivation covering the carrier, overlap matrix, and three projections (QM, GR, SM) plus connections to mathematics and physics domains. Multiple verification scripts and result files confirm every expression.</p>
    <p><span class="proof-badge">✓</span> Formally verified in Cubical Agda: <a href="https://github.com/andrey-kokoev/marici/blob/main/research/nima/agda/RelationalCarrier.agda" target="_blank">RelationalCarrier.agda</a> (107 proof files, composed). Lean: <a href="https://github.com/andrey-kokoev/marici/blob/main/research/buzzard/Entry2125.lean" target="_blank">probe kernel</a>, <a href="https://github.com/andrey-kokoev/marici/blob/main/research/buzzard/marici_formal/MariciFormal/PhysicsPredictions.lean" target="_blank">predictions (ℚ)</a>.</p>
    <a class="text-action" href="/theory/">Read the full relational carrier derivation &rarr;</a>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .section-heading { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }

  .dimension-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden; }
  .dt-role { padding: 12px 16px 4px; font: 700 .72rem/1.3 Consolas, monospace; letter-spacing: .13em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ochre); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-soft); }
  .dt-name { padding: 12px 16px 4px; font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 650; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-soft); }
  .dt-body { padding: 8px 16px 12px; color: var(--muted); font-size: .92rem; line-height: 1.5; }
